**An intruder selfie app takes a photo with the front camera when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password on your phone.** The main difference between the apps is what happens to that photo. CrookCatcher and Lockwatch email it to you with the phone's location, so you have it even if the phone is gone. WTMP and Third Eye keep it in a log on the phone, which suits you if you only want to know who touched your phone at home. Hidden Eye hasn't been updated since 2019.

We make CrookCatcher, so read this with that in mind. Everything below comes from each app's own Google Play listing as it was on November 13, 2026. Android has no intruder selfie feature of its own; here's [what Android does with failed unlock attempts](/blog/failed-unlock-attempts-android).

## The five apps compared

| | CrookCatcher | Lockwatch | WTMP | Third Eye | Hidden Eye |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Photo on wrong PIN | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Email alert | Yes | Yes | Not mentioned | Not mentioned | No |
| GPS location | Yes | Yes | Not mentioned | Not mentioned | No |
| Video | Pro | Premium | Not mentioned | Not mentioned | No |
| Ads | Yes | No | Not labeled | Yes | Yes |
| Downloads | 10M+ | 5M+ | 10M+ | 5M+ | 5M+ |
| Rating | 4.7 | 4.4 | 4.7 | 4.4 | 3.4 |
| Last updated | Nov 2026 | Jun 2026 | Oct 2026 | Aug 2026 | Oct 2019 |

"Not mentioned" means the app's Play listing doesn't describe the feature. All five are free to install. All except Hidden Eye offer in-app purchases.

## What matters when you choose

- **Where the photo goes.** A photo that's only saved on the phone is useless once the phone is stolen. For theft, you need an alert that leaves the phone: an email with the photo.
- **Location.** A photo shows who. A location shows where the phone was at that moment, which is what the police ask for.
- **Whether it's still maintained.** Android changes its rules for background apps and the camera almost every year. An app that hasn't been updated for years may quietly stop working on a new phone.
- **Lock screen or app lock.** Some "intruder selfie" features are part of an app lock and only react to a wrong password for one app. The five apps here all watch the phone's lock screen.

## 1. CrookCatcher

CrookCatcher takes a photo when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, and emails it to you with the GPS location and an estimated address. All of that is in the free version. It has been on Google Play since 2014 and takes around 1.2 million intruder photos a day.

Pro adds video with sound, a photo from the back camera, Google Drive backup, an alarm, a message on the lock screen, a fake home screen and a disguised app icon.

- **Good for:** theft and snooping alike, because the evidence leaves the phone.
- **Keep in mind:** the free version shows ads. It reacts to a wrong PIN, pattern or password, not to a failed fingerprint.

